# 02 — SMOTE Oversampling Analysis

This notebook implements imbalance-aware minority-class augmentation using the Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE).

The workflow investigates:

- minority-class sparsity,
- synthetic minority interpolation,
- class-distribution transformation,
- and imbalance-aware optimisation preparation.

Oversampling procedures are applied exclusively to training partitions in order to preserve evaluation integrity and minimise information leakage.

from imblearn.over_sampling import SMOTE

smote = SMOTE(
    sampling_strategy="auto",
    random_state=42
)

X_train_smote, y_train_smote = smote.fit_resample(
    X_train_scaled,
    y_train
)

print("Before SMOTE:")
print(y_train.value_counts())

print("\nAfter SMOTE:")
print(pd.Series(y_train_smote).value_counts())

plt.figure(figsize=(6,4))

sns.countplot(
    x=pd.Series(y_train_smote)
)

plt.title("Class Distribution After SMOTE")

plt.xlabel("Bankruptcy Class")
plt.ylabel("Observation Count")

plt.show()

# SMOTE Analysis Summary

SMOTE-based oversampling improved minority-class representation during optimisation by synthetically augmenting sparse bankruptcy observations.

The workflow preserves evaluation integrity by restricting oversampling exclusively to training partitions.

The transformed class distribution supports improved minority-event sensitivity during downstream ensemble learning optimisation.
